The devteam has always had a policy of never discussing upcoming
releases.  Pat Rankin is a devteam member, and occasionally mentions
an upcoming version.  The last time I remember this happening was when
NitroHack first came out when he did confirm that there really was a
new version in progress, but his dev environment made progress slow.

Other signs of life from the devream would be responses some have
gotten to bug reports, and occasional updates to official binaries to
remain compatible with the latest OS releases (e.g. the update for Mac
OS X Lion).

It used to be that talented individuals who had produced a number of
good patches would be recruited by the devteam.  I'm not sure if they
do that anymore, but I haven't heard any evidence of it if they do.
Good choices for recruitment are pretty obvious, and if they were to
get
help from some of the talented developers, I'm sure a new version
could
finally see the light of day, and probably be better than it otherwise
would have been.


